"Proofs of Some Kanade--Russell Mod 12 Conjectures | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2609.07822)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; The paper by Liuquan Wang provides the first analytical proofs for these remaining six conjectures, thereby completing the verification of the entire Kanade–Russell mod 12 family. ... This analytical proof confirms the numerical observations made by Kanade and Russell and establishes the identity as a theorem.\nSubmitted 07 Sept 2026\n\nen\n\n# Proofs of Some Kanade--Russell Mod 12 Conjectures\n\nLW\n\nLiuquan Wang\n\n## Abstract\n\nKanade and Russell conjectured seventeen Rogers--Ramanujan type identities of modulus 12. Eleven of these identities involving triple sums were proved by Bringmann--Jennings-Shaffer--Mahlburg and by Rosengren. Motivated by these works and using similar methods, we settle all the six remaining conjectures including two triple sum identities labeled $I_{5a}$I 5 a​ and $I_{6a}$I 6 a​ originated from Russell's thesis and four quadruple sum identities labeled 7, 7a, 8 and 8a. Our proof of the triple sum identities combines linear recurrences, $q$q-difference equations, and $q$q-series summation formulas. For the quadruple sum identities, we represent the sums as contour integrals whose integrands are infinite products and evaluate them by residue calculus. The resulting residues reduce to single sum cubic basic hypergeometric series, and we are able to express them as infinite products.\n\n## AI Overview\n## The Kanade–Russell Mod 12 Conjectures\n\nIn recent years, Shashank Kanade and Matthew C. Russell utilized computer-assisted experimental methods to search for new partition identities. Their search was specifically motivated by the level-two characters of the affine Lie algebra $A_9^{(2)}$A 9(2)​. This investigation yielded a set of 17 conjectural identities associated with modulus 12.\n\nBefore the publication of the current paper, 11 of these 17 conjectures had been rigorously proven. The remaining six identities were particularly elusive because of their complexity. They consist of two \"triple sum\" identities (labeled I5a and I6a) and four \"quadruple sum\" identities (labeled 7, 7a, 8, and 8a). The triple sums involve three indices of summation ($i$i, $j$j, $k$k), while the quadruple sums involve four ($i$i, $j$j, $k$k, $l$l).\n\nThe quadruple sum identities are particularly challenging because they do not easily reduce to simpler double or triple sums, a property that allowed previous researchers to prove other identities in the set. The paper by Liuquan Wang provides the first analytical proofs for these remaining six conjectures, thereby completing the verification of the entire Kanade–Russell mod 12 family.\n\n## Analytical Framework for Triple Sum Identities\n\nTo address the triple sum conjectures I5a and I6a, the author adopts a strategy based on $q$q-difference equations. This approach begins by embedding the sum into a more general function by introducing an auxiliary variable $x$x. For the identity I5a, the sum is expressed through a function $A(x)$A(x).\n\nThe technical heart of this method involves the $q$q-Zeilberger algorithm. This is a computer-algebraic procedure used to find linear recurrence relations for the coefficients of $q$q-series. By applying this algorithm, the author identifies a recurrence for the coefficients of $A(x)$A(x), which is then translated into a second-order $q$q-difference equation:\n\n$$A(x) = (1 + q^{-4} + x^2q^2 + x^2q^6)A(xq^3) - q^{-4}(1 + x^2q^7)(1 + x^2q^{11})A(xq^6)$$A(x)=(1+q−4+x 2 q 2+x 2 q 6)A(x q 3)−q−4(1+x 2 q 7)(1+x 2 q 11)A(x q 6)\n\nSolving such an equation requires expressing $A(x)$A(x) in terms of basic hypergeometric series. Specifically, the author shows that $A(x)$A(x) can be related to a $_2\\phi_1$2​ϕ 1​ series, a standard object in $q$q-calculus. The final step involves the use of the Bailey–Daum summation formula, which allows for the evaluation of specific $_2\\phi_1$2​ϕ 1​ series into an infinite product. For identity I5a, the resulting sum side is:\n\n$$\\sum_{i,j,k \\geq 0} (1 + q^{2i+4j+6k+2} - q^{3i+6j+9k+5}) q^{\\binom{i+2j+3k}{2} + i+j^2+2j+2k} \\frac{1}{(q; q)_i (q^2; q^2)_j (q^3; q^3)_k} = \\frac{1}{(q^2; q^3)_\\infty (q, q^6, q^9; q^{12})_\\infty}$$i,j,k≥0∑​(1+q 2 i+4 j+6 k+2−q 3 i+6 j+9 k+5)q(2 i+2 j+3 k​)+i+j 2+2 j+2 k(q;q)i​(q 2;q 2)j​(q 3;q 3)k​1​=(q 2;q 3)∞​(q,q 6,q 9;q 12)∞​1​\n\nThis analytical proof confirms the numerical observations made by Kanade and Russell and establishes the identity as a theorem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWe note that in some cases we slightly rewrite the product side of these conjectures. The conjectures for \\(H_{10}(1)\\) and \\(H_{11}(1)\\) are the original modulo 12 conjectures from [14], and the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1), H_{3}(1), H_{4}(1), H_{5}(1), H_{6}(1), H_{7}(1), H_{8}(1)\\), and \\(H_{9}(1)\\) are respectively identities 1, 2, 3, 4, 4a, 5, 5a, 6, and 6a of [15]. As alluded to above, the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1)\\), and \\(H_{3}(1)\\) arose from the principally specialized characters for \\(A_{9}^{(2)}\\) listed in (1.2), (1.3), and (1.4). The conjectures for \\(H_{4}(1)\\) through \\(H_{9}(1)\\) are asymmetric companions to the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1)\\), and \\(H_{3}(1)\\). (https://www.mi.uni-koeln.de/Bringmann/IdentitiesKanadeRussell.pdf)\nciteturn7search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.7 years ago; The conjectures for \\(H_{10}(1)\\) and \\(H_{11}(1)\\) are the original modulo 12 conjectures from [14], and the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1), H_{3}(1), H_{4}(1), H_{5}(1), H_{6}(1), H_{7}(1), H_{8}(1)\\), and \\(H_{9}(1)\\) are respectively identities 1, 2, 3, 4, 4a, 5, 5a, 6, and 6a of [15]. ... However, it is striking that all of the remaining conjectures contain asymmetric products, which have occurred infrequently in the classical theory of partitions (for example, Göllnitz’s so-called Big Theorem [9]).\n\nKanade and Russell also gave combinatorial interpretations for the sum-sides of each of these conjectures. ... In this article, we provide proofs of some of these conjectures and reductions of others.\nWe note that in some cases we slightly rewrite the product side of these conjectures. The conjectures for \\(H_{10}(1)\\) and \\(H_{11}(1)\\) are the original modulo 12 conjectures from [14], and the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1), H_{3}(1), H_{4}(1), H_{5}(1), H_{6}(1), H_{7}(1), H_{8}(1)\\), and \\(H_{9}(1)\\) are respectively identities 1, 2, 3, 4, 4a, 5, 5a, 6, and 6a of [15]. As alluded to above, the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1)\\), and \\(H_{3}(1)\\) arose from the principally specialized characters for \\(A_{9}^{(2)}\\) listed in (1.2), (1.3), and (1.4). The conjectures for \\(H_{4}(1)\\) through \\(H_{9}(1)\\) are asymmetric companions to the conjectures for \\(H_{1}(1), H_{2}(1)\\), and \\(H_{3}(1)\\).\n\n*Remark.* It is not surprising that the infinite products in (1.6), (1.7), and (1.8) are symmetric (in the sense that they consist of factors of the form \\((1-q^{n})^{\\pm1}\\) for all \\(n\\) in certain residue classes \\(\\pm r\\) modulo 12. Indeed, this follows from the Lepowsky-Milne numerator formula combined with the fact that in a finite-dimensional simple lie algebra the roots always occur in symmetric pairs \\((\\alpha,-\\alpha)\\). Furthermore, Kac and Peterson [12] showed that these products are essentially modular functions on certain congruence subgroups, which are expressible as the quotient of theta functions. However, it is striking that all of the remaining conjectures contain asymmetric products, which have occurred infrequently in the classical theory of partitions (for example, Göllnitz’s so-called Big Theorem [9]).\n\nKanade and Russell also gave combinatorial interpretations for the sum-sides of each of these conjectures. For example, the sum-side for \\(H_{1}(1)\\) generates all partitions such that if \\(\\pi_i\\) is a part then \\(\\pi_i+1\\) is not a part, odd parts do not repeat, and if \\(\\pi_i=\\pi_{i+1}\\) are even, then \\(\\pi_i-\\pi_{i-1}\\ge 4\\) and \\(\\pi_{i+2}-\\pi_i\\ge 4\\). The product side generates the partitions where each part is congruent to 1, 4, 6, 8, or 11 modulo 12, and the conjectured identity is then equivalent to the statement that for all \\(n\\) there are an equal number of partitions of both types. The remaining conjectures are similar, and were discussed in detail in [15]. These interpretations are not immediately apparent from the series (1.5), but instead require a careful combinatorial analysis in which a partition satisfying difference conditions is decomposed into a staircase and jagged component.\n\nIn this article, we provide proofs of some of these conjectures and reductions of others. In particular, we prove the following.\n\n**Theorem 1.1.** *Conjectures (1.6), (1.7), (1.8), (1.11), (1.12), (1.15), and (1.16) are true.*\n\nThis result is the first time that any of Kanade and Russell’s conjectures in [14] and [15] are proven. Our proofs use a variety of techniques, including summation and transformation formulas for hypergeometric \\(q\\)-series, series solutions to \\(q\\)-difference equations, and linear recurrences. The cases (1.6), (1.7), and (1.8) are perhaps the most significant, as these partition identities involve the principally specialized characters for \\(A_{9}^{(2)}\\), and are therefore likely to be useful in verifying the vertex operator construction of the corresponding standard modules (cf. [13]).\n\nAlthough we have not yet fully proven (1.9), (1.10), (1.13), or (1.14), the following result reduces the “sum-sides” from (1.5) to expressions involving a single hypergeometric \\(q\\)-series in these cases.\n\n**Theorem 1.2.** *The following identities are true:*\n\n\\[\nH_{4}(1)=(q^{3};q^{4})_{\\infty}\\sum_{n\\ge 0}\\frac{(q^{3};q^{6})_{n}q^{n^{2}}}{(q,q^{2};q^{2})_{n}(q^{3};q^{4})_{n}}, \\qquad (1.17)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nH_{5}(1)=(q^{-1};q^{4})_{\\infty}+(q^{3};q^{4})_{\\infty}\\sum_{n\\ge 0}\\frac{(1+q^{2n-4}+q^{2n-1})(q^{-3};q^{6})_{n}q^{n^{2}+4n+3}}{(1-q^{2n+2})(q^{-1},q^{2};q^{2})_{n}(q^{3};q^{4})_{n}}, \\qquad (1.18)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nH_{8}(1)=(q;q^{4})_{\\infty}+(q^{5};q^{4})_{\\infty}\\sum_{n\\ge 0}\\frac{(1+q^{2n}+q^{2n+1})(q^{3};q^{6})_{n}q^{n^{2}+2n+1}}{(1-q^{2n+2})(q,q^{2};q^{2})_{n}(q^{5};q^{4})_{n}}, \\qquad (1.19)\n\\]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRestrictions of free arrangements and the division theorem (https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.03863)\nciteturn7academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.7 years ago; Authors: Takuro Abe ... The division theorem can be regarded as a modified converse of the Orlik's conjecture with a combinatorial condition, i.e., an arrangement is free if the restriction is free and the characteristic polynomial of the restriction divides that of an arrangement.\nTitle: Restrictions of free arrangements and the division theorem\nAuthors: Takuro Abe\nDate: Sat Mar 12 05:11:26 2016\n\nThis is a survey and research note on the modified Orlik conjecture derived from the division theorem introduced in [2]. The division theorem is a generalization of classical addition-deletion theorems for free arrangements. The division theorem can be regarded as a modified converse of the Orlik's conjecture with a combinatorial condition, i.e., an arrangement is free if the restriction is free and the characteristic polynomial of the restriction divides that of an arrangement. In this article we recall, summarize, pose and re-formulate some of results and problems related to the division theorem based on [2], and study the modified Orlik's conjecture with partial answers.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPROOFS AND REDUCTIONS OF VARIOUS CONJECTURED PARTITION (https://www.math.lsu.edu/~mahlburg/preprints/37.pdf)\nci